fact WorkItem(id: "WI-927", description: some(value: "A sort that is BOTH eponymous (§6.3: `sort Box { entity Box(value: T) }`) and PARAMETERIZED (`sort T = ?`) cannot be written as a bracketed type application in VALUE position. MEASURED at WI-926's HEAD, control vs subject in one probe: a NON-eponymous `sort Holder { sort T = ?; entity mk(value: T) }` loads `rule ok(?x) :- eq(?x, is_modifiable(Holder[T = Int64]))` clean, while the same rule over an eponymous `Box` is REFUSED with `UnknownEntityField { entity: \"Box\", field: \"T\", declared: \"value\" }`. TYPE position is unaffected -- `entity use(b: Box[T = Int64])` loads CLEAN, because an annotation lowers through `TypeExpr::Parameterized` and never reaches the Apply/Constructor decision. ROOT CAUSE: the bracketed `X[..]` and parenthesized `X(..)` surfaces reach `LoadBuildFrame::ApplyOrConstructor` as ONE node -- `is_type_application` is parse-time provenance that load.rs:9052 documents as the only thing that can tell the two apart, and it IS consulted at the three `check_sort_type_args` sites, but it is NOT carried onto the built `Expr` (every `Expr::Apply` is built with an empty `type_args` at the ApplyOrConstructor drain). Once §6.3 makes one symbol both the sort and the entity, the `is_entity` decision at that drain can only pick one reading, and it picks CONSTRUCTION -- correct for the common case, and LOUD (not silent) for this one. The defect is therefore a missing CHANNEL, not a wrong choice. NOT A REGRESSION IN REACH: before WI-926 the shape was unreachable for a different reason (the eponymous entity had a separate nested symbol), and no source in the repo exercises it -- the stdlib's three eponymous sorts (TotalFloat, Duration, Timestamp) are all non-parameterized. FIRST STEP: decide whether the bracket surface gets its own occurrence channel (carry `is_type_application` onto the Expr, the load.rs:9052 reading) or whether an eponymous parameterized sort is refused AT DECLARATION as an unrepresentable combination (the make-illegal-states-unrepresentable reading). ACCEPTANCE: either a bracketed value-position type application over an eponymous parameterized sort loads and denotes the type, or the combination is refused at its declaration with a located diagnostic; the kernel-language.md §6.3 bullet recording today's behaviour updated to match; cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-926"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-02T05:56:56Z"))

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-927", author: "user", content: "Resolved by the FIRST option (give the bracket surface its own reading), not the second (refuse the combination at declaration). Refusing would have banned a generic record type -- `sort Box { sort T = ?; entity Box(value: T) }` is exactly the shape anthill-cpp-gen's `generic_entity_emits_template_prefix` emits a `template<typename T>` for -- so the \"unrepresentable combination\" reading would have removed a working capability to avoid a reading bug. WIDER THAN THE TICKET MEASURED. The ticket recorded the NAMED form (`Box[T = Int64]` refused as `unknown field 'T'`). Driving found the POSITIONAL form was worse and SILENT until the very end: `Box[Int64]` reached the WI-433 positional->named desugar, which filled the entity's FIRST FIELD with the type argument, so `check_sort_type_args` then reported \"`Box` has no type parameter named 'value'\" -- a label the author never wrote, describing a rename the loader had just done. Also found: the stray case DOUBLE-REPORTED (`Box[W = Int64]` produced both `UnknownEntityField{W}` and `InvalidTypeArgument{W}`), so one written node yielded two errors under two readings. FIX SITES, both producers, both using provenance parse ALREADY records (`mark_type_application`) -- no new mechanism: (1) `convert_term`'s `Term::Fn` arm binds `is_type_app` ONCE and skips the entity-field reading (the positional->named desugar and the unknown-label check); the sibling `check_sort_type_args` gate now reads that same binding instead of re-asking parse, so the gate and the reading it excludes cannot disagree. (2) The `ApplyOrConstructor` occurrence drain (operation bodies) makes `is_entity` false for a bracketed node. The generalization is not eponymous-specific: a BRACKET IS NEVER A CONSTRUCTION, for any functor. What the ticket called \"a missing CHANNEL, not a wrong choice\" was right -- but the channel already existed at parse; it simply was not consulted at these two sites, because the functor's KIND stood in for the surface, which worked only while a sort and an entity were always distinct symbols (pre-WI-926). MEASURED MATRIX after the fix, each eponymous case paired with a non-eponymous control of the same shape: `Box[T = Int64]` clean (was `unknown field 'T'`); `Box[Int64]` clean (was \"no type parameter named 'value'\"); `Box[W = Int64]` reports ONLY `no type parameter named 'W'` (was a double report); `Box(value: 1)` constructs; `Box(valu: 1)` still reports `'Box' has no field 'valu'`; `b: Box[T = Int64]` in type position unchanged. Control run with the fix disabled: 3 of the 5 new tests fail; the other 2 are must-not-change controls that hold both ways by design. kernel-language.md 6.3's bullet, which recorded the pre-fix behaviour, is replaced by the surface rule.", at: "2026-08-02T05:56:53Z")
